# Coelho: a tour

```elixir
Mix.install([
  # Until 0.17 is on Hex, from GitHub.
  {:coelho, github: "nseaSeb/coelho"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:phoenix_live_view, "~> 1.1"}
])
```

## The editor

This is the editor a Phoenix form gets from `<.coelho_editor>`: the same
component, the same hook, the same schema on both sides. Type in it, select a
word and make it bold, start a line with `# ` or `- `.

```elixir
sample = %{
  "type" => "doc",
  "content" => [
    %{
      "type" => "heading",
      "attrs" => %{"level" => 2},
      "content" => [%{"type" => "text", "text" => "Structured rich text"}]
    },
    %{
      "type" => "paragraph",
      "content" => [
        %{"type" => "text", "text" => "Coelho stores "},
        %{"type" => "text", "text" => "a tree", "marks" => [%{"type" => "bold"}]},
        %{"type" => "text", "text" => ", never HTML."}
      ]
    }
  ]
}

editor = Coelho.Kino.new(value: sample)
```

## What is stored, and what it renders to

Every change is validated on the server before it is kept, then rendered again
from the tree: the JSON a `:map` column holds, the HTML a page shows, and the
plain text a search index would hold. Edit above and watch it follow.

```elixir
frame = Kino.Frame.new()

show = fn document ->
  Kino.Layout.tabs(
    "Stored JSON": Kino.Markdown.new("```json\n#{JSON.encode!(document)}\n```"),
    Rendered: Kino.HTML.new(Coelho.to_html(document)),
    "HTML source": Kino.Markdown.new("```html\n#{Coelho.to_html(document)}\n```"),
    "Plain text": Kino.Text.new(Coelho.to_text(document))
  )
end

Kino.Frame.render(frame, show.(Coelho.Kino.read(editor)))

Kino.listen(editor, fn
  %{type: :change, document: document} -> Kino.Frame.render(frame, show.(document))
  _other -> :ok
end)

frame
```

## Reading it from Elixir

`read/1` is the document as a form would submit it, already validated.

```elixir
document = Coelho.Kino.read(editor)

%{
  characters: Coelho.text_length(document),
  blank?: Coelho.blank?(document),
  inline: Coelho.to_inline_html(document)
}
```

## The server decides

What the browser sends is input like any other. A document the schema refuses
is not stored, whoever sent it, and `set/2` says why:

```elixir
hostile = %{
  "type" => "doc",
  "content" => [
    %{
      "type" => "paragraph",
      "content" => [
        %{
          "type" => "text",
          "text" => "click me",
          "marks" => [%{"type" => "link", "attrs" => %{"href" => "javascript:alert(1)"}}]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{:error, errors} = Coelho.Kino.set(editor, hostile)
Enum.map(errors, &Coelho.Document.Error.format/1)
```
